a) The Secretary or designee will:

       1) Provide guidance on compliance to USDA Mission Areas, agencies, and staff
          offices regarding the implementation of Section 508 of the Rehabilitation Act;

       2) Ensure that funding is available to support the USDA Section 508 Program Office
          for the personnel and the purchasing of needed tools (e.g., Section 508 remediation,
          website monitoring and testing, document and training testing, software
          automation);

       3) Ensure that funding is available to support collaboration with Mission Areas,
          agencies, and staff offices on the purchase of proposed ICT enterprise-approved
          solutions to support accessibility;

       4) Designate a senior-level executive as the USDA Section 508 Official, 7 CFR §
          2.32(a)(12)(viii) delegates the Office of the Chief Information Officer (OCIO),
          Chief Information Officer (CIO) as the 508 Official;

       5) Ensure that Section 508 discrimination complaints are forwarded to the Assistant
          Secretary for Administration (ASA), Office of the Secretary (OSEC), and the CIO
          upon request; and

       6) Ensure that submission of reports and survey data related to ICT are provided to
          appropriate Government organizations. These include, for example, Congress, the
          Senate Committee on the Budget, and the House Committee on Oversight and
          Reform.

  b) The ASA will:

       1) Serve as the USDA Chief Acquisition Officer (CAO);




                                             8
     2) Implement Section 508 standards in the overall IT acquisition strategy and IT
       contract portfolio for the Department;

    3) Review the IT acquisition process in coordination with the USDA CIO and the
       Director, Office of Contracting and Procurement (OCP), serving as the Senior
       Procurement Executive (SPE) to ensure that Section 508 requirements are met;

    4) Delegate policy and oversight aspects of IT acquisitions to the Director, OCP, as it
       relates to Section 508 compliance and accessibility;

    5) Address other CAO responsibilities as outlined in the FAR (see Federal Acquisition
       Regulation: Section 508-Based Standards in Information and Communication
       Technology) to ensure that Mission Areas, agencies, and staff offices comply with
       Section 508 in their acquisitions of ICT; and

    6) Address the Section 508 contract language in contracts when needed or upon
       request.

c) The USDA CIO will:

    1) Provide oversight for the USDA Section 508 Program as delegated by the Secretary
       per 7 CFR § 2.32(a)(12)(viii);

    2) Serve as the USDA Section 508 Official;

    3) Designate the USDA Section 508 PM;

    4) Maintain and provide leadership to the USDA Section 508 Program.

    5) Provide guidance to the USDA Section 508 PM;

    6) Provide policy management, coordination, and oversight of the design, acquisition,
       maintenance, use, and dissemination of ICT;

    7) Ensure that Section 508 accessibility considerations are incorporated into the
       planning, operation, and management of any ICT that the Department develops,
       uses, or maintains;

    8) Ensure that funding and resources are available for the USDA Section 508 Program
       Office and its activities;

    9) Ensure that Section 508 is incorporated into all acquisition and development
       processes in OCIO in collaboration with the CAO;




                                           9
      10) Review and approve all Section 508 exception requests. Ensure that an appropriate
         response is provided for each unless delegated to the OCIO Information Resource
         Management Center (IRMC) Associate Chief Information Officer (ACIO);

     11) Collaborate with the Secretary or Designee and other senior USDA officials on
         matters pertaining to the implementation of Section 508 requirements and the
         resolution of Section 508 complaints;

     12) Develop and implement Departmental policy to ensure that the Section 508
         requirements are integrated with, but not limited to, DR 3130-013, Information
         Technology Capital Planning and Investment Control; DR 3130-010, United States
         Department of Agriculture Enterprise Information Technology Governance; and
         DR 3145-001, Oversight and Management of the Federal Information Technology
         Acquisition Reform Act (FITARA);

     13) Provide Section 508 conformance information in response to the biennial (every
         other year) Department of Justice (DOJ) Section 508 survey to OMB and DOJ;

     14) Provide Section 508 conformance information in response to the annual OMB
         Governmentwide Section 508 Assessment Report to OMB and DOJ; and

     15) Monitor and report USDA compliance on the USDA Section 508 Program
         regarding operational activities to OMB and the White House.

d) Mission Area, Agency, and Staff Office Heads will:

     1) Ensure senior executive leadership and accountability for the implementation of the
        Section 508 requirements in their respective program offices;

     2) Designate a Mission Area, agency, or staff office Section 508 Coordinator and
        communicate the selection to the USDA Section 508 PM in writing;

     3) Support the implementation of Section 508 in the development, acquisition,
        maintenance, use, and management of ICT products and services within their
        organization;

     4) Ensure that funding and resources are available to use towards Section 508
        compliance and managerial activities;

     5) Review all Section 508 exception requests to ensure that an appropriate response is
        provided for each and records are properly maintained;

     6) Ensure that Section 508 is incorporated into acquisition and developmental
        processes;




                                          10
      7) Ensure that only Section 508 compliant ICT or ICT with a documented exception
        operates in the USDA production environment;

     8) Ensure that employees and contractors within their Mission Area, agency, and staff
        office complete Section 508 training as required; and

     9) Update or reconfirm the Section 508 Coordinator’s appointment letter annually, or
        more often as needed.

e) The USDA Office of the General Counsel (OGC) will:

     1) Provide legal advice to the Secretary of Agriculture, senior officials, Mission Areas,
        agencies, and staff offices on questions of law involving Section 508 across all
        USDA;

     2) Provide legal advice on compliance with Section 508; and

     3) Advise and defend USDA, as needed, to address Section 508 complaints.

f) The Director, OCP, will:

     1) Serve as the USDA Senior Procurement Executive (SPE);

     2) Ensure, in collaboration with the CIO, that Section 508 requirements are
        incorporated into any ICT that is procured by the Department;

     3) Ensure that individuals involved in all financial planning and requirements
        generation develop plans and specifications that address ICT accessibility using the
        Section 508 standards addressed in the FAR;

     4) Include Section 508 contract language and any required supporting documentation
        for all ICT acquisitions. Apply due diligence as part of the market research
        requirements; and

     5) Use GSA’s Accessibility Requirements Tool (ART) as a resource to locate Section
        508 compliant products, services, and accessibility-related documentation for the
        contract file.

g) The Office of the Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights (OASCR) will:

     1) Ensure that Mission Areas, agencies, and staff offices conduct internal reviews to
        ensure their policies and procedures comply with Section 508 regulations,
        mandates, and amendments;

     2) Develop, implement, and maintain procedures for processing complaints of
        discrimination filed with OASCR by Federal employees, applicants for employment



                                           11
        at USDA, and members of the public. These procedures also apply to all
       complaints alleging a violation of the agency’s responsibility to procure ICT under
       Section 508 whether filed by members of the public or USDA employees or job
       applicants;

    3) Ensure procedures are consistent with 7 CFR § 2.25, Assistant Secretary for Civil
       Rights. This section defines the ASCR’s delegated authority. The procedures must
       also conform with 7 CFR Part 15e, Enforcement of Nondiscrimination on the Basis
       of Handicap in Programs or Activities Conducted by the United States Department
       of Agriculture;

    4) Maintain a system to track, document, and monitor Section 508 informal and formal
       complaints received by OASCR;

    5) Ensure Section 508 complaints received by OASCR are timely and properly
       processed;

    6) Evaluate the effectiveness of OASCR’s procedures for processing Section 508
       informal and formal complaints and implement changes as needed; and

    7) Provide reports and survey data on the number and status of Section 508 informal
       and formal complaints received by OASCR to the appropriate Federal agencies,
       OMB, and Congress.

h) The ACIO, OCIO-IRMC, will:

    1) Serve as acting USDA Section 508 Official, upon delegation from the CIO;

    2) Monitor the impact, needs, and performance of the USDA Section 508 Program
       Office;

    3) Implement Section 508 compliance within USDA;

    4) Provide leadership and guidance to the USDA Section 508 PM;

    5) Identify and evaluate current and future accessibility tools and technology for use
       across the Department;

    6) Identify and document ICT deficiencies impacting the performance of individuals
       with disabilities in both current and future prospective ICT systems;

    7) Establish enterprise system and accessibility testing protocols; and

    8) Advise the Secretary and other senior USDA officials on matters pertaining to the
       implementation of Section 508 requirements and the resolution of Section 508
       complaints.



                                          12
 i) The Director, OCIO-IRMC Enterprise Architecture Division will:

     1) Serve as the USDA Chief Enterprise Architect (CEA);

     2) Confirm that ICT acquisitions, investments, and projects follow the USDA Section
        508 Program or the Access Board standards and guidelines;

     3) Collaborate with the USDA Section 508 Program to promote Section 508
        awareness; and

     4) Ensure that Section 508 is integrated into DR 3185-001, Enterprise Architecture.

j) The Director, OCIO IRMC Information Management Division (IMD), will:

     1) Provide leadership and oversight for the USDA Section 508 Program and guidance
        to the USDA Section 508 PM;

     2) Collaborate with the Director, OCIO IRMC Capital Planning and IT Governance
        Division, to ensure that DR 3130-013 and DR 3130-010 incorporate Section 508
        standards;

     3) Monitor the impact, needs, and performance of the USDA Section 508 Program
        Office;

     4) Advise the USDA CIO and other senior USDA officials on matters pertaining to the
        implementation of Section 508 requirements and the resolution of Section 508
        complaints;

     5) Assist with identifying and evaluating current and future tools and technology for
        use across the Department;

     6) Assist with identifying and documenting ICT deficiencies impacting the
        performance of individuals with disabilities in both current and future prospective
        ICT systems; and

     7) Establish enterprise systems and accessibility testing protocols that support the
        assessment, reporting, and remediation of Section 508 violations.

k) The Director, OCIO IRMC Capital Planning and IT Governance Division, will:

     1) Ensure that DR 3130-013 and DR 3130-010 incorporate Section 508 standards;

     2) Ensure that USDA functional business areas, along with their IT investments,
        systems, and applications deliverables, are Section 508 compliant and accessible;




                                           13
     3) Ensure that AgMax has Section 508 contract language in procurement submission
       before approval; and

    4) Ensure that the IT governance process and procedures incorporate Section 508
       standards.

l) The Director, OCIO IRMC FITARA, will:

    1) Ensure that DR 3145-001 incorporates Section 508 standards;

    2) Ensure that all applicable policies, procedures, and governance structures are
       consistent with the Section 508 standards for accessibility; and

    3) Ensure that the IT investment processes and procedures incorporate Section 508
       standards.

m) The USDA Section 508 PM will:

    1) Manage the USDA Section 508 Program Office;

    2) Assist in the establishment of a strong and inclusive USDA Section 508 Program
       foundation across USDA;

    3) Oversee USDA Section 508 Program activities and abide by established standards;

    4) Host reoccurring meetings as necessary with Mission Area, agency, and staff office
       Section 508 Coordinators and accessibility professionals to provide updates,
       training, and information pertaining to Section 508;

    5) Coordinate the identification and remediation of noncompliant ICT operating in a
       production environment;

    6) Educate USDA employees and contractors on the importance of Section 508 as it
       applies to the acquisition, development, maintenance, and use of ICT;

    7) Establish procedures for conducting semiannual reviews of approved exceptions to
       ensure compliance with Section 508;

    8) Update this DR and the Section 508 Procedures in accordance with USDA policy;

    9) Implement standards, policies, and guidelines pertaining to Section 508-related
       activities across USDA;

    10) Monitor and report on Section 508 compliance in USDA Mission Areas, agencies,
        and staff offices;




                                          14
      11) Identify current and prospective ICT deficiencies impacting individuals with
         disabilities;

     12) Develop and maintain standardized Section 508 contract language to be used in
         solicitations and related procedures;

     13) Ensure that individuals involved in all financial planning and requirements
         generation develop plans and specifications that address ICT accessibility using the
         Section 508 standards addressed in the FAR;

     14) Review and respond appropriately to Section 508 exception requests;

     15) Routinely monitor the Access Board and the GSA websites for updated Section 508
         guidance, training opportunities, and best practices;

     16) Collect, organize, and submit Section 508 conformance metrics and information
         pertaining to each Mission Area, agency, and staff office in response to the annual
         OMB Governmentwide Section 508 Assessment Report;

     17) Collect, organize, and submit Section 508 conformance metrics and information
         pertaining to each Mission Area, agency, and staff office in response to the biennial
         DOJ Section 508 survey;

     18) Collaborate with OGC, OASCR, and the CEA to support Section 508 initiatives;

     19) Advise the CIO and other senior USDA officials on matters pertaining to the
         implementation of Section 508 requirements and the resolution of Section 508
         violations;

     20) Ensure that Section 508 program manager tasked with testing and evaluating digital
         accessibility have appropriate expertise and have, at a minimum, participated in a
         certified Section 508 conformance training program, such as the Department of
         Homeland Security (DHS) Trusted Tester Certification Program;

     21) Represent USDA at Federal meetings, conferences, and training sessions on Section
         508, as appropriate; and

     22) Participate in the Accessibility Community of Practice (ACOP) to maintain
         compliance with laws and stay current with the latest developments, issues, skills,
         and new technology requirements for compliance and accessibility.

n) Mission Area, Agency, and Staff Office Section 508 Coordinators will:

     1) Define and oversee Mission Area, agency, and staff office Section 508 program
        activities, including the strategic implementation of Section 508 procedures in order
        to meet Departmentwide Section 508 and accessibility standards;



                                           15
 2) Develop guidelines for how ICT is procured, developed, deployed, and used in
   accordance with Section 508;

3) Execute Section 508 activities related to training, acquisition, ICT evaluations,
   exception requests, and complaint consultations;

4) Provide technical assistance on Section 508 issues within their Mission Area,
   agency, or staff office;

5) Support PMs in reviewing completed vendors’ Information Technology Industry
   Council (ITIC) VPAT® and ACR generated by GSA’s ART within the business
   component;

6) Track and report Mission Area, agency, or staff office Section 508 conformance
   metrics as required by the USDA Section 508 PM in response to the OMB
   Governmentwide Section 508 Assessment Report and the DOJ Section 508 survey;

7) Track, review, and respond appropriately to Section 508 exception requests;

8) Communicate Section 508-related issues and concerns to the USDA Section 508
   PM;

9) Evaluate ICT against the Section 508 conformance standards. Provide remediation
   guidance to vendors, development teams, and content creators where applicable;

10) Coordinate the identification of noncompliant ICT in the USDA production
    environment;

11) Ensure the development and completion of remediation plans for ICT identified as
    noncompliant with Section 508;

12) Ensure that the Section 508 contract language provided by the USDA Section 508
    PM and any required artifacts are included in solicitations;

13) Ensure all coordinators tasked with testing and evaluating digital accessibility have
    appropriate expertise and have, at a minimum, participated in a certified Section
    508 conformance training program, such as the Department of Homeland Security
    (DHS) Trusted Tester Certification Program;

14) Represent their Mission Area, agency, or staff office at Federal meetings,
    conferences, and training sessions for Section 508 as appropriate; and

15) Participate in meetings and events held by the USDA Section 508 Program Office
    to receive updated information and guidance pertaining to Section 508.




                                       16
 o) Acquisition, Management, and Procurement Officials (Contracting Officers) will:

     1) Incorporate standardized Section 508 contract language and all related artifacts in
        solicitations for all ICT procurements;

     2) Enforce contract compliance with all applicable Section 508 contract provisions;

     3) Advise procurement package creators on the Section 508 acquisition requirements;

     4) Ensure that the results from market research and the documentation for approved
        exceptions, if applicable, are maintained in the contract files for all ICT
        procurements; and

     5) Ensure that all ICT procurements are evaluated for Section 508 conformance.

p) Project Managers will:

     1) Conduct market research in accordance with USDA policy and procedures to
        identify Section 508-compliant ICT that meets the business need;

     2) Use the ART as a resource to locate Section 508-compliant products, services, and
        vendor voluntary product accessible templates for the contract file;

     3) Include the Section 508 contract language and supporting documentation for all ICT
        acquisitions and apply due diligence as part of the market research requirements;

     4) Ensure that all ICT procurements are evaluated for Section 508;

     5) Review the Access Board and GSA standards to determine the applicable Section
        508 standards for procured and developed ICT products and services;

     6) Determine and ensure that the applicable Section 508 standards are defined early in
        development life cycles;

     7) Determine, request, and document any applicable Section 508 exceptions in
        accordance with the USDA Section 508 exception process detailed in the Section
        508 Procedures;

     8) Develop and implement appropriate remediation plans for noncompliant ICT
        operating in the production environment;

     9) Ensure that newly developed or procured ICT is either Section 508 compliant or has
        a documented and approved exception granted prior to use in the production
        environment; and




                                           17
         10) Ensure that vendors provide complete and accurate responses to the Section 508
            standards for all ICT being developed, procured, maintained, or used by USDA
            employees and contractors and the public.

   q) Developers, Content Creators, Software Engineers, and Webmasters will:

        1) Incorporate the applicable Section 508 standards throughout development life
           cycles;

        2) Design, develop, and implement ICT services with Section 508-compliant and
           accessible user interfaces;

        3) Create, maintain, evaluate, and remediate accessible ICT and electronic content in
           accordance with Section 508 and the current WCAG standards; and

        4) Develop and implement remediation plans for known accessibility-related issues in
           accordance with USDA procedures.

   r) USDA Employees and Contractors performing work on behalf of USDA will:

        1) Complete the mandatory Section 508 training annually;

        2) Properly apply Section 508 provisions to relevant work activities;

        3) Engage with the respective Section 508 Coordinator throughout the acquisition,
           development, and authoring processes of all ICT;

        4) Provide evidence of accessibility conformance when required;

        5) Adhere to the requirements of this USDA policy, and its associated standards and
           procedures, when developing, procuring, maintaining, or using ICT; and

        6) Ensure that all content, documents, applications, and files authored, owned,
           developed, maintained, and submitted for publication on USDA public-facing
           websites, on internal USDA platforms and the USDA intranet, or distributed via
           broadcast emails, for which they are responsible, are Section 508 compliant per the
           Section 508 standards.
